          "parts": [
            {
              "text": "For a separation involving **complex property division** (such as corporate entities, family trusts, self-managed super funds (SMSFs), private businesses, or high-value residential/commercial real estate) and **Binding Financial Agreements (BFAs)**, engaging a specialist is critical. \n\nUnder Sections 90G and 90UJ of the *Family Law Act 1975*, BFAs carry strict compliance requirements—if drafted without precise technical execution and independent advice, they are vulnerable to being set aside by the court.\n\nWhile there are local suburban firms across Indooroopilly, Kenmore, and Mount Ommaney, matters involving high-net-worth pools and intricate business structures are best served by **Queensland Law Society (QLS) Accredited Specialists in Family Law** who frequently appear in peer-reviewed benchmarks such as *Doyle’s Guide for High-Value & Complex Property Matters*. \n\nThe firms below hold the strongest track records in Brisbane for high-asset property divisions and technical financial agreements:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Premier Specialist Firms for Complex Property Settlements\n\n* **Phillips Family Law**\n  * **Focus:** Consistently ranked in the *First Tier* in Queensland by *Doyle’s Guide* for high-value and complex property disputes. \n  * **Track Record:** Tony Phillips and Fiona Caulley are regarded as authorities in matters involving multi-tiered corporate/trust structures, commercial interests, and post-separation tax structuring (e.g., Division 7A, capital gains tax rollovers). They have an extensive history of drafting robust BFAs.\n* **Barry Nilsson**\n  * **Focus:** One of Australia’s premier dedicated family law teams, headquartered in Brisbane.\n  * **Track Record:** Partners Geoff Sinclair, Scott Wedgwood, and James Steel are regularly cited at the top tier of complex family property matters. They routinely handle asset pools involving commercial partnerships, equity portfolios, and cross-border or interstate assets.\n* **HopgoodGanim Lawyers**\n  * **Focus:** Full-service firm with a dedicated family law practice (led by practitioners such as Geoff Wilson and Alison Ross).\n  * **Track Record:** Ideal for business owners, executives, or individuals with large corporate balance sheets. Because they are a full-service commercial firm, their family lawyers work alongside their in-house corporate and tax teams, which is a major advantage when unwinding joint companies, shareholder agreements, or commercial leases.\n* **Cooper Grace Ward Lawyers**\n  * **Focus:** Highly regarded Brisbane commercial firm with a dedicated family law division (headed by Justine Woods).\n  * **Track Record:** Known for handling sophisticated property division where estate planning, family trusts, and commercial tax issues overlap. They are experienced in both drafting BFAs and advising on risk management where agreements are contested.\n* **Damien Greer Lawyers**\n  * **Focus:** Boutique specialist family law practice.\n  * **Track Record:** Damien Greer is an Accredited Specialist with over four decades of experience focused specifically on high-net-worth financial disputes, forensic asset tracing, and high-stakes financial agreements.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Inner-West & Western Suburbs-Accessible Specialists\n\nFor consultations closer to the Western Corridor (without traveling into the CBD core):\n\n* **Macpherson Family Law (Milton / Inner-West)**\n  * Led by Don Macpherson, one of Queensland’s first Accredited Specialists in Family Law. He focuses heavily on complex financial disputes and brings strong commercial insight to property settlements that involve closely held family companies.\n* **Daykin Family Law (Services Western Suburbs / Inner-West)**\n  * A boutique family law practice that specifically targets property divisions, BFAs, and de facto separations across Indooroopilly, St Lucia, and surrounding western suburbs.\n* **Stewart Family Law (Brisbane / Western Access)**\n  * Judy Stewart is a leading mediator and family lawyer with extensive experience in drafting airtight BFAs and managing complex parenting/property matters through private mediation.\n\n---\n\n### What to Look For When Interviewing Firms for a Complex Property Matter\n\n1. **QLS Family Law Accreditation:** Verify that the lead solicitor or partner managing your matter is an **Accredited Specialist in Family Law** (this demonstrates peer-reviewed technical proficiency beyond general practice).\n2. **Experience with Accounting & Forensic Experts:** Complex pools (common in Centenary/Indooroopilly executive and business-owner households) often require single-expert or shadow business valuations, add-back analyses, and division of SMSFs. Ask the firm about their familiarity with local valuation and forensic accounting practices.\n3. **Approach to Binding Financial Agreements (BFAs):** BFAs require strict statutory disclosure. Ask the lawyer:\n   * *How often do they draft post-separation BFAs vs. Consent Orders?* (Sometimes Court Consent Orders are a safer or more tax-effective instrument for property transfers than a BFA, depending on whether spousal maintenance is wrapped into the split).\n   * *What steps do they take to ensure full and frank disclosure is documented to prevent the agreement from being vulnerable to future challenge?*\n4. **Resolution Strategy:** While you need a firm with courtroom strength in the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (FCFCOA), look for one that emphasizes alternative dispute resolution (ADR), arbitration, or private mediation first, as this will protect private business records from the public forum.",
